WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.575
As you might already know,

2
00:00:01.575 --> 00:00:04.155
you can interact with
the database using SQL.

3
00:00:04.155 --> 00:00:06.450
But just like with
other coding languages,

4
00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:08.475
you need experience
with SQL syntax

5
00:00:08.475 --> 00:00:11.025
and it's subsets before
we can make use of it.

6
00:00:11.025 --> 00:00:12.735
Over the next few minutes,

7
00:00:12.735 --> 00:00:15.270
you'll learn how to
create a database using

8
00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:19.275
the data definition language
or DDL subset of SQL.

9
00:00:19.275 --> 00:00:21.915
Utilize the data
manipulation language,

10
00:00:21.915 --> 00:00:24.090
also known as the DML subset,

11
00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:25.950
to populate and modify data in

12
00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:28.620
a database and read
and query data within

13
00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:31.335
databases using the
Data Query Language

14
00:00:31.335 --> 00:00:33.495
or DQL subset of SQL.

15
00:00:33.495 --> 00:00:36.945
In order to demonstrate
SQL syntax and at subsets,

16
00:00:36.945 --> 00:00:39.180
I'm going to show you the
SQL commands that can be

17
00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:41.460
used to develop a
database for college.

18
00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:44.630
However, take note that the
demonstration which follows,

19
00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:47.345
will only briefly show
each step in the process.

20
00:00:47.345 --> 00:00:49.100
You just need to
develop a working

21
00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:50.995
familiarity with SQL for now.

22
00:00:50.995 --> 00:00:53.120
You'll explore the
language in a subsets

23
00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:55.445
in much more detail
later in this program.

24
00:00:55.445 --> 00:00:58.115
The first task is to
create the database.

25
00:00:58.115 --> 00:00:59.840
To do this, I already create

26
00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:02.845
statement using SQL DDL subset.

27
00:01:02.845 --> 00:01:07.190
The syntax to create a
database is create database,

28
00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:09.410
followed by the name
of the database.

29
00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:12.560
I then place the semicolon
at the end of the statement.

30
00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:15.290
Let's create a college
database as an example using

31
00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:18.225
the syntax, create
database college.

32
00:01:18.225 --> 00:01:20.525
Once you've created a database,

33
00:01:20.525 --> 00:01:22.745
the next step is to
create the tables.

34
00:01:22.745 --> 00:01:24.440
You can create tables using

35
00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:27.635
the create table syntax
followed by the table name.

36
00:01:27.635 --> 00:01:29.600
Just repeat the same steps for

37
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:32.470
each new table you want
to add to your database.

38
00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:34.520
I can use this syntax to create

39
00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:36.800
a student table in
my college database.

40
00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:39.860
This table will hold
information on each student.

41
00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:44.410
To create the table, I just
write create table student.

42
00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:47.225
Now I need to populate
the table with data.

43
00:01:47.225 --> 00:01:48.650
This is where I can use

44
00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:52.180
the data manipulation language
or DML subset of SQL.

45
00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:53.615
To add table data,

46
00:01:53.615 --> 00:01:56.455
I use the insert into syntax.

47
00:01:56.455 --> 00:01:59.735
This inserts rows of
data into a given table.

48
00:01:59.735 --> 00:02:02.030
I just type insert into,

49
00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:03.470
followed by the table name,

50
00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:05.270
and then a list of
required columns

51
00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:07.330
or fields within a
pair of parentheses.

52
00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:09.650
Then I add the
values keyword and

53
00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:13.250
specify in order the values
for each of the fields.

54
00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:15.290
As an example, let's add data to

55
00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:17.750
the student table in
our college database.

56
00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:20.840
I'll use the student table
I created earlier and add

57
00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:22.130
student data to it by

58
00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:24.935
specifying values for each
of the following columns.

59
00:02:24.935 --> 00:02:27.170
ID, first name,

60
00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:29.590
last name, and date of birth.

61
00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:32.685
Then populate the table
with the required data.

62
00:02:32.685 --> 00:02:35.750
But what if I need to
update or modify data?.

63
00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:37.490
For example, let's say I've

64
00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:39.920
input the wrong date of
birth for a student.

65
00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:41.405
To change this data,

66
00:02:41.405 --> 00:02:43.145
I can use the update syntax,

67
00:02:43.145 --> 00:02:45.725
which is part of the
DML subset of SQL.

68
00:02:45.725 --> 00:02:48.020
First I add the update keyword,

69
00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:50.150
followed by the
student table name.

70
00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:52.205
Then I use the set keyword

71
00:02:52.205 --> 00:02:54.935
followed by columns and
values I want to update,

72
00:02:54.935 --> 00:02:56.830
written as key-value pairings.

73
00:02:56.830 --> 00:02:58.970
In this instance is a date of

74
00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:01.730
birth column and a new
date of birth value.

75
00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:03.890
Finally, I add the where clause

76
00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:06.355
and a condition to filter
the records I need.

77
00:03:06.355 --> 00:03:08.480
For example, to change the data

78
00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:10.430
for the student
with the ID of two,

79
00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:14.090
I can type where ID equal to 2.

80
00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:17.195
It's also possible to
delete data from a table.

81
00:03:17.195 --> 00:03:19.760
Let's delete the table
record for the student with

82
00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:22.805
the ID of three using
the delete syntax.

83
00:03:22.805 --> 00:03:27.025
First I type delete from,
then the table name.

84
00:03:27.025 --> 00:03:30.840
This tells my SQL where the
data must be deleted from.

85
00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:32.780
This is followed by
the where clause and

86
00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:35.585
a condition such
as ID equal to 3,

87
00:03:35.585 --> 00:03:38.810
which will remove all data
on row 3 of the table.

88
00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:41.240
I can instruct my SQL to remove

89
00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:43.660
the data of the
student on row 3.

90
00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:45.350
Once I run this statement,

91
00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:47.885
the students data is
removed from my table.

92
00:03:47.885 --> 00:03:49.940
You're now familiar
with how to add,

93
00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:52.675
update or delete
data in a database.

94
00:03:52.675 --> 00:03:54.050
But how would you read data

95
00:03:54.050 --> 00:03:56.075
stored in your database tables?

96
00:03:56.075 --> 00:04:00.325
That's where SQL, DQL or data
query language comes in.

97
00:04:00.325 --> 00:04:03.375
The main syntax
of DQL is select.

98
00:04:03.375 --> 00:04:05.210
As its name says, it's used to

99
00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:07.040
select data from the database.

100
00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:10.505
A select statement is written
using the select keyword,

101
00:04:10.505 --> 00:04:13.640
followed by the columns that
hold the data you required.

102
00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:16.220
You then write them from keyword

103
00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:17.660
followed by the
name of the table

104
00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:19.610
you want to select data from.

105
00:04:19.610 --> 00:04:22.550
As an example, you could
use the select statement

106
00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:24.950
to query the student
table to find the name of

107
00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:27.740
the student with an
ID of one You just

108
00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:29.390
need to add the where keyword

109
00:04:29.390 --> 00:04:31.175
followed by the student's ID.

110
00:04:31.175 --> 00:04:34.130
This will then return
the name of John Murphy.

111
00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:36.320
You're now familiar
with the basics of

112
00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:38.500
SQL syntax and the subsets.

113
00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:40.130
Don't worry if
you're still trying

114
00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:41.660
to figure out these subsets.

115
00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:43.130
You will explore each of them in

116
00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:45.290
more depth later in
this specialization.

117
00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:48.840
You'll also get an opportunity
to try them for yourself.